    Completely wrong I'm afraid.  I had a pair of VAX6620's and a VAX6420
that would all boot from a TK70.  Built stand-alone BACKUP for them, but
also "built by hand" a tape with console firmware to upgrade the CIXCD's
on the 6620's to do the 4K packets.  That was interesting...but worked!

    All the VAX 7x0's booted from tape; so di d the 8600 and 8700
(probably the 8800 as well, but don't have direct experience with that).
The VAX 7000's being of a later generation _may_ not boot from tape, at
least not by default (they typically would boot off an Infoserver),
and the 9000, while I've seen one, I simply don't know.

    It was mainly the smaller "workstation" VAXes that didn't boot
from tape.  Well...the VAXstation 2000 would, if you had a tape drive
for it, though most booted it as a satellite instead.  In fact, I can't
think of a VAXstation that wouldn't boot from tape if you had one.
Only the very last series, like the "souped-up" 4000-96, etc., which
were of a generation that _might_ have had a CD installed, might not
have been particularly easy to boot from tape.  The main thing is that
VMS stopped being distributed on tape in the V7.0 time frame, if not
earlier.

    Come to think of it, I can't think of _any_ VAX that you can't
build STABAKIT on.  Alphas are different and absolutely (almost)
require a CD drive.  But I believe every VAX can boot from a stand-
alone backup tape, which is all you need to do a VMS installation.

    -Ken
