Ben,
     actually, you were quite helpful last year when I was getting an
overdrive or equivalent for this box.  I ended up with a Powerleap
adapter for the K6-2, which was their best unit that would work on a
socket-5 with the older BIOS (ie no K6-3 available).  With an
aftermarket copper heat sink and fan unit attached using heat transfer
adhesive tape, it all fits nicely inside the case (no hole!) and runs
reliably for days (running benchmarks, not real work!) at 330 MHz (at
366 or 400 it dies reliably within minutes of booting).  The best the
stock low profile cooler would do was 233MHz, in part I think because
they used a 1cm square thermal tape square to hold it; I used a square
the size of the metal cap on the K6-2!

     The few NT compatible games I have from 1996-1997 timeframe run
fine on it, but they aren't really suitable for my 9 year old nephew
(some are kiddie games from his last visit!)  Unfortunately support
for NT in games is almost nonexistent, even when the hardware reqs
aren't so far out of this little box's league.  But most of the
el-cheapo rack and many older games should do just fine on this
hardware under win98 2nd. as long as directx of appropriate vintage
will run on the embedded video (which is an unknown.)  The sound issue
is an unknown; the posts online seem to imply that it just 'didn't
work' under 98, and I never saw anyone post a working solution.

     Guess its worth a try; there's nothing on the NT disk worth
keeping, and I can always do a dual boot by reinstalling NT later.  Of
course I could use this as an excuse to by a PSII too!

     So will win2000 (< professional) run in 128MB on a 330MHz K6-2?

Rich

