On Sat, 4 May 2002, JF Mezei wrote:

> Terry C Shannon wrote:
> > In status quo mode, I'd say VMS will be on life support sometime within
> > the next five years.
>
> 5 years from now, how will Unix compare with VMS in terms of reliability and
> clustering ? Would it be able to take on most of the VMS missions, with the
> remainder going to Tandem ?

Possible, but I wouldn't want to assign a "probability factor" to it. Note
that a lot of TruCluster goodies have a VMScluster heritage. Prior to the
plans for the "Consolidaed Enterprise UNIX," Compaq planned to outfit
Tru64 UNIX with online system component add and removal (CPUs today,
storage and memory in the future), automatic removal of failing memory
and CPU components, full dynamic system partitioning, TruCluster process
management and migration, and finally software fault tolerance via process
pairing and failover.


The result would have been a pretty darned capable product. Capable,
perhaps, of snagging some low-hanging fruit in the NSK realm.


What with Tru64 UNIX becoming an organ donor to HP-UX, it's unknown how
many of the aforementioned Tru64 roadmap plans remain viable.

terry s



