  The last VAX microprocessor CPU was fabricated a number of years ago, 
  and the last (new) VAX systems shipped out somewhere between (circa) 
  1999 and 2001, depending on the particular module.  Please see:

    http://www.compaq.com/alphaserver/vax/index.html

  DIGITAL sold the Hudson semiconductor fabrication line to Intel some 
  years ago, and DIGITAL and Compaq and most other system vendors utilize 
  an outsourced module, contracting the semiconductor fabrication to other 
  vendors -- IBM is (AFAIK) the only system vendor that maintains 
  semiconductor fabrication abilities, most vendors outsource their
  fabrication requirements.

  Compaq has further announced that EV7 (21364) and EV79, and the systems 
  based on these microprocessors, will be produced.  Subsequently, there
  is a migration to the Intel Itanium Processor Family.  Details on the 
  announcement are also available, via a link at:

    http://www.openvms.compaq.com/

  OpenVMS is presently being ported to the Itanium Processor Family, and 
  OpenVMS Alpha and OpenVMS IA-64 will share a common code base. 

  Terminology: Itanium is an implementation of the IA-64 architecture -- 
  DIGITAL and Compaq have traditionally used the platform names and the 
  architecture names largely interchangeably.

