"Peter da Silva" <peter@abbnm.com> wrote in message
news:aardoq$s02@web.eng.baileynm.com...
> Possibly it's time to abandon the "Shannon Knows Foo" naming scheme, or
> make it "Shannon Doesn't Know What To Call This Site"?

Quite possibly so. Truth be known, it wasn't me who came up with the
original naming scheme. Back in the 80's, there was a newsletter called
Monosson on DEC. It got bought by DataTrends Publications (Sonny Monosson
laughed all the way to the bank), who hired me to write a monthly column in
what they initially called the Datatrends Report on DEC, thence Terry
Shannon on DEC. That publication ultimately folded, but DataTrends retained
the naming rights. Which meant that Jonathan Eunice and I had to come up
with another name for the newsletter we wanted to launch. T'was Jonathan or
his significant other that conjured up "Shannon Knows..." I thought the name
was a bit arrogant and pretentious, but I couldn't come up with a better
one. After all, Digital News, Digital Review, Digital News & Review, DEC
Pro, VAX Pro, Hardcopy, DEC Computing, OnDEC, Computers R Digital, etc all
were taken!

Life was so much easier back in 1964 when Pat McGovern launched
ComputerWorld (ne Computerworld, the InterCappIng came later). A nice
all-purpose name that's withstood the test of time, not to mention myriad
mergers, acquisitions, and bankruptcies.




