It seems to me that what you need to decide is if it is important to
continue to carry the name of the company that you are covering in your
publication title - given that has 'morphed' again.  So, carrying forward
Shannon Knows HP makes sense... if you plan to expand it to embrace the rest
of HP (printers, HP-UX, etc).  Using "HPS" is sort-of a nice little cheat -
you get the HP in, but the HPS limits the scope.  But of course, when IBM
aquires HP in 2015, you'll have to change it again ;-)

You could decide that at this point, it is your name that people buy (and
that they know what is inside) and rename your newsletter to something like
"The Shannon Report" - which isn't much more pretentious - and decouples the
company from your newsletter.

Or you could go for real focused - Shannon Knows VMS ;-)



